WEBVTT

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.880
I suppose you could call this program a travelogue.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.180
It does travel through space and time.

00:00:39.250 --> 00:00:41.720
Or you could call it a kind of detective story.

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:43.630
It's certainly full of mysteries.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.000
Or you could say it's about one of history's greatest cases of mistaken identity.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.920
Or you could call it any one of these things.

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.050
After all, what's in a name?

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:04.030
Here we are in the Mysterious Orient, right?

00:01:04.500 --> 00:01:07.100
Colourful, exotic customs, ancient rituals.

00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:10.050
Above all, perhaps, different.

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:14.590
Well, that's what it says in the brochure.

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.950
Fact is, that might have been true 50 years ago.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:24.540
But today, half the world cities, including Singapore, look more like Dallas.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:29.960
And anyway, nowadays, most travellers and holidaymakers don't actually want all that.

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:38.270
foreign stuff, do they? I mean, ask anybody in a package tour. Ah, room service. Thank you. What

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:43.660
most people care about when they're abroad is the state of their insides. So you also stick to

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:50.580
international food. Well, that way you don't get Montezuma's Revenge or Deli Belli or whatever you

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:57.820
want to call it. Well, not with this you don't. The new global diet for the new global person.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:01:59.970
The calorie-aware, weight...

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:06.490
conscious, healthy living individual. This little flake is eaten by hundreds of millions

00:02:06.490 --> 00:02:16.700
of people worldwide. Pretty much anywhere you go, you can get cornflakes. Now, there

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:22.180
are those gourmets and old-fashioned exploring adventurers who lament the passing of local

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:29.420
customs in things like food and the disappearance of exotic breakfasts, of fried ants, boiled

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:29.940
tripe.

00:02:30.570 --> 00:02:34.080
Black pudding, meat soup, cheese and olives,

00:02:34.630 --> 00:02:36.250
or whatever people used to eat

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:40.480
before this new high-speed diet for a high-speed world.

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:44.460
There are even those who would say that,

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:47.090
gastronomically speaking, this is a load of garbage,

00:02:47.230 --> 00:02:48.920
which would be unfair.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:51.740
There is garbage associated with it,

00:02:52.150 --> 00:02:54.070
but I assure you it comes into the story

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:56.010
well after you've finished your cornflakes.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:58.730
So let me bring it into the story now.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:02.460
This is what I'm talking about.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.660
What's left after you've taken the corn for the cornflakes?

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:08.240
There are thousands of tons of it being produced every day.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.600
Ah, used corn cobs.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.700
And thanks to a totally forgotten German chemist,

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:17.040
who I think really didn't know what he was doing anyway,

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.080
this garbage is far from garbage.

00:03:20.660 --> 00:03:23.380
For much of modern industry, what you can do with this stuff

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:25.480
really beats the band.

00:03:34.280 --> 00:03:37.100
This musical interlude is happening in the market square

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:40.140
of the small eastern German town where the story starts.

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:43.520
It's called Jena, and back in 1810,

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:45.920
it's got one of the best universities in the country

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:49.300
and two professors, one of whom becomes world famous.

00:03:49.540 --> 00:03:51.640
The other one's totally forgotten.

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.680
Until this programme drags him out of obscurity

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:57.030
thanks to those corn cobs I mentioned.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:03:59.940
But let's have a little more oompa.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:25.980
first hmm okay sorry back to corn cobs again while I lead you up the garden path in what can

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:29.920
only be described as the corniest story I've ever told

00:04:31.500 --> 00:04:37.410
One of the things in the 19th century you're not going to find in fancy botanical gardens like this is that.

00:04:38.020 --> 00:04:42.520
I mean, from a horticultural point of view, this is pretty low-rent stuff.

00:04:43.540 --> 00:04:48.010
Back then, you feed corn cobs to cows and chickens, you grow mushrooms on them,

00:04:48.250 --> 00:04:51.910
you use them to fill swampy ground, and sometimes you make pipes out of them.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:53.940
What are you going to do with corn cobs?

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:59.960
And then in 1810, the fellow who lives in this...

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:05.640
house and runs this botanical garden and administers Jena University and is one of those

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:12.320
academics I mentioned, the one who becomes world famous. The great German poet Goethe takes

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:21.620
up the cause of a corncob and changes history. Thanks to encouragement from Goethe, an associate

00:05:21.620 --> 00:05:26.240
professor of chemistry here that I bet my life you've never heard of, called Wolfgang

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:29.710
Dobreiner, takes a close look at various organic matters.

00:05:30.250 --> 00:05:31.810
and comes up with a totally new chemical,

00:05:31.810 --> 00:05:35.790
I also bet my life you've never heard of, called furforal.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:41.400
Now, don't ask me, or him, why De Bruyne should do such a thing.

00:05:41.520 --> 00:05:44.500
He just does, and then does nothing about it,

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:48.000
except to write the usual humongously boring scientific paper,

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:52.300
about which, for 100 years, nobody else does anything either.

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:59.980
But wait, here we are in the 1930s, and still nobody cares about...

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.020
poor old De Bruyne's furforal, until people start drilling oil wells all over the place.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:06.820
Bad news for farmers.

00:06:07.450 --> 00:06:10.790
Till this point, most useful chemicals came from plants.

00:06:11.210 --> 00:06:14.250
But now, they're going to be petroleum by-products.

00:06:14.910 --> 00:06:19.580
Agri-business better find some new way to use their leftovers, or they'll be.

00:06:23.300 --> 00:06:26.860
One day, the people at Quaker Oats dig up De Bruyne's stuff

00:06:26.860 --> 00:06:29.960
and discover that if you press, boil, steam and shove...

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:34.680
acid into corn cobs and other kinds of organic garbage left after people have had their breakfast

00:06:34.680 --> 00:06:40.130
what do you get but furfural which now turns out to be a chemical that goes into all kinds

00:06:40.130 --> 00:06:51.840
of good stuff so from the dim recesses of academic life to brine his little discovery ends up in

00:06:51.840 --> 00:06:58.320
every chemical library in the world one of the great solvents of all time used in the production

00:06:58.320 --> 00:06:59.460
of stuff like petroleum.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.120
products, synthetic rubber, insecticides, herbicides, and all kinds of pharmaceuticals,

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:10.140
lubricants, weed killers, and something in quantum physics I don't understand. Oh, and

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:17.400
one thing De Bruyne would really have appreciated, anti-carbuncle cream, which, fortunately for

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:23.360
you, is not the next connection in this programme, because the other place Firfaral gets used

00:07:23.510 --> 00:07:29.640
is in making a resin that is just what people want for bonding abrasives to grinding wheels.

00:07:30.640 --> 00:07:33.000
A subject you've waited all your life to know about.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:35.380
I know, so I won't make light of it.

00:07:39.480 --> 00:07:42.300
The light in question is a quarter of a million bulbs,

00:07:42.540 --> 00:07:46.940
lighting up the great Chicago-Columbia Exposition of 1893,

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:50.280
all thanks to George Westinghouse,

00:07:50.300 --> 00:07:53.780
who gets around Edison's patent for a one-piece light bulb

00:07:53.780 --> 00:07:55.900
with a two-piece light bulb.

00:07:56.520 --> 00:07:59.700
Bottom, an open-necked bulb, into which...

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.060
Above, the filament base fits.

00:08:02.630 --> 00:08:04.570
You plug it into the open neck of the bulb.

00:08:05.060 --> 00:08:07.840
And you maintain the vacuum inside the light bulb

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:09.400
because everything's airtight,

00:08:09.800 --> 00:08:12.760
thanks to the ground glass collar that fits perfectly.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:21.400
Ah, meet abrasive American Edward Goodrich Acheson,

00:08:22.290 --> 00:08:24.790
who does all that ground glass work for Westinghouse

00:08:24.790 --> 00:08:28.250
because, thanks to an accident, he owns the Carborundum Company.

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:33.180
The accident happens earlier, in 1861,

00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:37.940
when Acheson is bashing a lot of electricity into a mixture of coke and clay.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:41.770
At a humongously high temperature, the whole lot melts.

00:08:42.510 --> 00:08:43.090
Big deal.

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:46.790
And certainly not the abrasives Acheson's trying to make at the time.

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:51.380
And then he notices some tiny crystals in the clay.

00:08:52.870 --> 00:08:54.590
He puts a bit on the end of a pencil lead,

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:57.220
and it scratches glass like diamonds.

00:08:58.130 --> 00:08:58.630
Bingo!

00:08:59.510 --> 00:08:59.970
Well...

00:09:00.220 --> 00:09:02.040
Where else would he go but here?

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:09.510
Now, if there's one industry that needs good abrasives,

00:09:09.720 --> 00:09:11.500
it's the diamond business,

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:14.280
harder and more polished than which there isn't.

00:09:15.270 --> 00:09:18.140
Back then, Tiffany's is where the bejeweled elite meet

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.480
to choose their sparklers.

00:09:19.770 --> 00:09:22.530
So, not surprisingly, it's somebody at Tiffany's

00:09:22.530 --> 00:09:25.650
who encourages Atchison to do something about his accident,

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:27.770
like do it again on purpose,

00:09:28.160 --> 00:09:29.980
and market the world's hardest...

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:37.260
material after diamonds, carborundum. Chemical name, silicon carbide. Carborundum is soon being

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:43.320
described as an indispensable tool of industry by anybody with anything that needs an edge,

00:09:44.760 --> 00:09:53.520
like plough shares. And speaking of plough shares, swords. One spectacular modern use of

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:59.900
Atchison silicon carbide happens in 1960s Vietnam with the invention of an outfit called the air.

00:10:00.120 --> 00:10:04.780
cavalry, whose choppers are highly manoeuvrable, well-armed and everywhere.

00:10:09.040 --> 00:10:15.600
Now, taking the wounded out or dropping green berets in, delivering supplies, doing reconnaissance

00:10:15.600 --> 00:10:20.940
or conducting rocket attacks, chopper people are constantly and acutely aware of one minor

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:21.380
problem.

00:10:22.260 --> 00:10:27.670
One of the reasons choppers are so clever is because they can sit still in midair, which

00:10:27.670 --> 00:10:29.890
kind of makes them a target it is difficult to

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:36.140
miss. So it's the chopper people who think up a use for Atchison silicon carbide as a protective

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:43.150
lining for their vulnerable machines. Thing being, silicon carbide's a lot tougher than most of the

00:10:43.150 --> 00:10:58.000
stuff shooting up at them from the jungle below. One of the nicest things you can say to somebody

00:10:58.000 --> 00:10:59.940
fighting a war on the ground in the jungle.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.750
is put this on and you won't get hurt so what starts up there with the chopper

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:10.480
pilots very soon comes down here with the grunts where the fighting is very close

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:20.650
quarters spoil your day type of stuff so in no time at all but the well-dressed

00:11:20.650 --> 00:11:25.510
dog face is wearing is some helicopter protection for humans called a flak

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:29.980
jacket which has some very interesting features to it like if you

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.640
wearing one, you can throw a grenade just a few yards, turn and hunch up, and survive

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:39.960
your blast while others do not. And more than half of all wounds that were previously lethal

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:45.520
become no longer lethal. You still get a real pain in the chest, maybe, but that's a lot

00:11:45.520 --> 00:11:49.940
better than getting taken seriously dead by somebody's armour-piercing ordinance. Speaking

00:11:49.940 --> 00:11:55.590
of which, let me give you some hard facts. It all started with cornflakes, made from

00:11:55.590 --> 00:11:59.910
corn cobs that De Bruyne attended to Firforal to bond silicon carbide.

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.920
abrasives that Westinghouse used to grind light bulbs, and that also ended up as protection

00:12:05.920 --> 00:12:12.660
for choppers and in flak jackets to protect against armor-piercing ammunition, which originally

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:17.410
got developed for use against something that first appeared in World War I.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:29.360
The main snag for World War I infantry is getting snagged.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.120
then shot on rows of barbed wire. So here's the solution. Named because it looks just like

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:44.150
a water tank, a tank. Main job, roll over and flatten the barbed wire so the troops can

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:49.510
get through okay, and then act as protection for the troops as they go on into the advance.

00:12:50.770 --> 00:12:56.710
Small wonder the tank soon becomes everybody's favourite war machine, except for the people

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.560
inside where it can get up to 125

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:07.170
degrees cramped and smelly. But what a ride. It can handle six foot vertical slopes and

00:13:07.220 --> 00:13:18.980
15 foot vertical drops. Only question is, can the crew? Tanks are a smash success and

00:13:18.980 --> 00:13:23.960
nothing can stop them except, as I said, the armour-piercing ammunition, which is now invented.

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:29.970
Meanwhile, tanks win the war for the Allies. In 1918, 15...

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:33.000
59 British divisions beat 99 German divisions

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.530
because the Brits have tanks and the Germans have no chance.

00:13:37.510 --> 00:13:41.050
Tanks also offer a bright future to one particular bunch of soldiers

00:13:41.050 --> 00:13:44.950
who take them over, the old sabre-wielding cavalry

00:13:44.950 --> 00:13:47.870
and, speaking of swords, plowshares.

00:13:54.630 --> 00:13:57.030
Now, if you take a close look at this picture,

00:13:57.390 --> 00:13:59.970
you'll see two things that caused the tank to happen.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:07.590
in the first place. The soft soil here in the beautiful San Joaquin Valley, California,

00:14:09.400 --> 00:14:15.120
that and the fact that all the horses are about to be taken off the farms to be sent to Europe

00:14:15.120 --> 00:14:29.060
for the war effort. This critical situation inspires an enterprising type called Ben Holt,

00:14:29.360 --> 00:14:29.980
who lives here.

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.560
in the San Joaquin Valley, to invent one of those amazing labour-saving devices

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:39.560
for which America has always been famous, set the stage for the development of the tank

00:14:39.560 --> 00:14:43.620
and change the whole nature of farming, here on the local fields,

00:14:43.780 --> 00:14:47.470
and then all over the world, virtually at a stroke.

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:55.570
Introducing the machine that turns America into the world's breadbasket

00:14:55.570 --> 00:14:59.780
and solves the problem of horselessness, the horseless tractor.

00:15:01.080 --> 00:15:02.720
Take a look at this wonderful thing.

00:15:32.040 --> 00:15:36.290
It won't surprise you that one of Holt's pals watching this thing at work

00:15:36.510 --> 00:15:38.410
says it reminds him of a caterpillar.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.300
So guess what the company ends up being called?

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:44.130
And the rest is farm equipment history.

00:15:45.120 --> 00:15:46.880
But let me give you a quick tour of the place.

00:15:48.480 --> 00:15:52.320
Number one, the secret of the Holt success, the crawler tracks.

00:15:53.000 --> 00:15:57.520
The trick here is the track spreads the load all the way along the track

00:15:57.520 --> 00:15:59.900
so the weight of the machine isn't concentrated.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.060
on the wheels. And what that means is, there's nowhere too soft for this baby to go. Which

00:16:06.060 --> 00:16:10.360
is great news for farmers here in the San Joaquin Valley and then everywhere else.

00:16:11.160 --> 00:16:16.950
Number two, a wheel at the front. So if it rolls down into a hole, it rolls up out the

00:16:16.950 --> 00:16:23.380
other side. Number three, Holt drives each track separately. So what that means is if

00:16:23.380 --> 00:16:29.210
you want to change direction, you just do this. Turn on a dime. Well, a quarter.

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:31.760
But you can see why the generals back in Europe

00:16:31.760 --> 00:16:33.120
jump at the idea, though.

00:16:33.680 --> 00:16:36.180
You can use one of these things to haul army supplies

00:16:36.180 --> 00:16:39.710
night and day, thick and thin, and it doesn't need hay.

00:16:40.110 --> 00:16:43.100
And you can also turn it into a tank, which they do.

00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:45.440
Well, that's World War I in the bag.

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:46.670
Drinks all round.

00:16:48.400 --> 00:16:51.090
One last thing about tractors, their engines,

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:52.910
which is why I mentioned the drink.

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.700
Beer, as it happens, because that's what it takes

00:16:56.700 --> 00:16:58.320
for Holt and everybody else in the US

00:16:58.320 --> 00:16:59.720
to get to hear about diesel engines.

00:17:00.730 --> 00:17:06.120
You know, history's funny in some ways, because the fellow who helps to make tractors synonymous with diesel engines

00:17:06.120 --> 00:17:09.840
is a brewer from St. Louis called Gustavus Adolphus Busch.

00:17:10.440 --> 00:17:12.400
You can still buy his stuff today, see?

00:17:13.170 --> 00:17:17.330
Anyway, Busch buys the US rights to diesel engines in 1897

00:17:17.740 --> 00:17:23.900
because the technical director at his brewery is a German who was at school with a fellow called Rudolf Diesel.

00:17:29.200 --> 00:17:29.960
And Diesel...

00:17:31.280 --> 00:17:36.950
gets his first job with a fellow who kind of invents refrigeration and he does that to keep

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:44.780
beer cool anyway diesel lonely childhood grows up obsessed with mechanical efficiency what can

00:17:44.780 --> 00:17:51.460
i say it takes all sorts besides it does end up making rudolph rich and famous so let's hear it

00:17:51.460 --> 00:17:58.190
for mechanical obsessions now the really clever thing about a diesel engine is it's not a gasoline

00:17:58.190 --> 00:17:59.980
engine. And the reason that...

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:10.360
clever can only be described as a lot of hot air. First because a gasoline engine compresses the air

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:16.000
and the fuel spray mix and then a spark explodes the mixture. So you have to get the timing just

00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:21.310
right or the spark will happen when the mixture is not ready. And if you let the air compress too

00:18:21.310 --> 00:18:27.540
much, it'll ignite the fuel spontaneously. But that's just the point with a diesel engine.

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:29.770
Here, there's no mixture.

00:18:30.060 --> 00:18:31.910
Just air, which you compress.

00:18:32.640 --> 00:18:36.620
And when you do, its temperature rises to about 8,000 degrees Celsius.

00:18:38.620 --> 00:18:43.720
At the top of the cycle, at maximum pressure and temperature, you spray in some fuel.

00:18:43.960 --> 00:18:47.730
The hot air ignites it and pushes the piston down.

00:18:48.680 --> 00:18:51.700
What you're getting here is extremely efficient use of fuel

00:18:51.700 --> 00:18:54.860
because a very high compression gives you more bang for your buck.

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:58.490
And because it's so efficient, you save fuel.

00:18:59.670 --> 00:18:59.970
That does.

00:19:00.000 --> 00:19:07.240
is save you money. But the really brilliant thing about Diesel's idea is

00:19:07.240 --> 00:19:10.160
that his engine will run on almost anything. And that's great news for

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:13.860
Europeans, because since the engine doesn't explode the fuel the way a

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:18.160
gasoline engine does, it doesn't need refined petroleum product. And that's

00:19:18.160 --> 00:19:22.820
good, because back then in Europe, with almost no oil industry to speak of, that

00:19:22.820 --> 00:19:29.970
kind of stuff costs an arm and a leg. Anyway, sometime around 1899...

00:19:30.000 --> 00:19:33.900
In 1973, diesel gets a contract from a big engine factory here in Germany.

00:19:34.220 --> 00:19:40.310
And in no time at all, diesel engines are in everything from battleships to fancy cars.

00:19:43.850 --> 00:19:46.950
And what makes all this happen for a man with a lonely childhood

00:19:46.950 --> 00:19:51.080
is the fact that one of the fuels diesel says the engine might be able to use,

00:19:51.180 --> 00:19:52.780
there's lots of in Germany.

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:54.380
It's coal.

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:58.370
So all you have to do is dig a big hole.

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:06.620
Now, there's one man who owns more coalmines than anybody else in Germany.

00:20:07.280 --> 00:20:10.290
And shipyards, steelworks, armaments factories,

00:20:10.610 --> 00:20:12.930
and practically the whole German industrial machine.

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:16.380
What am I saying? He is the whole German industrial machine.

00:20:16.970 --> 00:20:19.170
