of it up here. There's not much else. Now, when you're talking about whether a country

00:40:25.370 --> 00:40:29.910
makes it to industrial status, which Scotland now does, as you saw, thanks.

00:40:30.000 --> 00:40:38.020
to Nielsen, having those iron foundries is the litmus test, which is what Nielsen's new partner

00:40:38.020 --> 00:40:54.930
Charles McIntosh and his father George here have been doing in the Scottish glens. Because litmus

00:40:54.930 --> 00:40:59.970
paper does all that stuff, like changing colour when you dip it in acid or alkali, if you

00:41:00.000 --> 00:41:05.200
recall your school chemistry lessons, because it's impregnated with this stuff, a particular

00:41:05.200 --> 00:41:15.600
lichen or moss that George McIntosh is looking for. The moss is one ingredient of a dye called

00:41:15.600 --> 00:41:22.390
cud beer, which will colour cloth bright red. So, since back in 1777, George McIntosh,

00:41:22.530 --> 00:41:27.820
who dyes cloth, has been spending weeks at a time out on romantic moss-collecting trips

00:41:27.820 --> 00:41:29.970
among the heather to get his raw material.

00:41:30.000 --> 00:41:48.760
Well, most unfortunately, Cadbier dye needs one other vital ingredient besides Scottish moss,

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:55.230
and it's something that will take us back from these lonely highlands to that hot air gas company manager, Nielsen,

00:41:55.490 --> 00:41:59.980
because the other ingredient for Cadbier dye requires the Macintosh company to...

00:42:00.000 --> 00:42:01.720
to go looking for raw materials

00:42:01.720 --> 00:42:05.070
in less salubrious places than the Scottish glens.

00:42:05.510 --> 00:42:08.530
It's urine, which contains ammonia,

00:42:09.160 --> 00:42:11.320
which is why Nielsen and the McIntosh family

00:42:11.320 --> 00:42:12.820
got together in the first place,

00:42:13.120 --> 00:42:16.160
since ammonia is a by-product of what you get

00:42:16.160 --> 00:42:17.540
when you make coal gas.

00:42:17.720 --> 00:42:21.420
And you'll recall that's what Nielsen made at his gas works.

00:42:23.090 --> 00:42:26.450
OK, let's leave George McIntosh up this mountain

00:42:26.450 --> 00:42:27.970
while I change the subject,

00:42:28.480 --> 00:42:29.960
because at this point in the story...

00:42:30.000 --> 00:42:32.300
the path of history splits into two.

00:42:33.100 --> 00:42:35.940
The first pathway takes us away from moss collecting

00:42:35.940 --> 00:42:39.700
to some pretty weird goings-on a good deal farther south.

00:42:40.320 --> 00:42:42.280
In India, to be exact.

00:42:50.560 --> 00:42:53.320
This is one of those isn't-it-a-small-world stories.

00:42:54.140 --> 00:42:56.970
Back in Scotland, Nielsen's father has been employed

00:42:56.970 --> 00:42:59.550
by a fellow called John Roebuck, who owns coal mines.

00:43:00.030 --> 00:43:03.830
And Roebuck has been taught in Edinburgh by a professor called Joe Black.

00:43:05.090 --> 00:43:08.050
Roebuck tells Black about the water flooding his mines

00:43:08.050 --> 00:43:11.250
and Black tells Roebuck about James Watt of steam engine fame

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:15.040
and together Watt and Roebuck make mind-draining music

00:43:15.040 --> 00:43:17.360
with Watt's new steam-driven pump.

00:43:23.820 --> 00:43:26.580
Roebuck's money sets Watt up in business and he makes a fortune

00:43:26.580 --> 00:43:29.960
and eventually becomes an amateur scientist like all gentlemen did.

00:43:30.000 --> 00:43:38.460
at the time. Thank you. Now, one of Watts' experiments causes a bit of a stir in the

00:43:38.460 --> 00:43:43.020
hallowed halls of the British Royal Society when he crosses swords with an aristocrat

00:43:43.020 --> 00:43:47.380
called Lord Henry Cavendish. They're always all about which one of them has done some experiment

00:43:47.380 --> 00:43:54.830
or other to find out what water is. Well, Watts becomes a member of the Royal Society. The

00:43:54.830 --> 00:43:57.070
two of them meet and become friends, and that's that.

00:44:00.280 --> 00:44:05.510
However, at the time, Cavendish is also sponsoring to the society a young man.

00:44:06.120 --> 00:44:07.640
That's why I'm telling you all this in India,

00:44:07.660 --> 00:44:10.950
because the young man is a fellow called James Macy.

00:44:11.220 --> 00:44:15.160
And his main contribution to science is to investigate...

00:44:15.600 --> 00:44:16.080
Hang on a minute.

00:44:20.520 --> 00:44:24.100
To investigate a liquid that appears in the joints of bamboo.

00:44:24.560 --> 00:44:27.440
Because in 1791, some of this mystery liquid

00:44:27.440 --> 00:44:29.480
has been sent back to England from India.

00:44:30.000 --> 00:44:32.340
And out here, it's used as an eye ointment.

00:44:32.340 --> 00:44:33.850
And, well, you never know.

00:44:35.300 --> 00:44:38.240
So friend Macy takes this medicinal bamboo juice

00:44:38.240 --> 00:44:40.080
and does everything but drink it.

00:44:40.320 --> 00:44:42.620
Under differing circumstances, it effervesces,

00:44:42.880 --> 00:44:45.800
turns jelly-like, goes into a glass shape,

00:44:45.820 --> 00:44:47.200
or becomes a powder.

00:44:48.800 --> 00:44:52.480
Now, there is no point in asking why anybody would do all that to this.

00:44:52.880 --> 00:44:53.720
He just does.

00:44:54.520 --> 00:44:57.240
The upshot is, Macy manages to get the liquid

00:44:57.240 --> 00:44:59.680
to turn into something resembling a flint pebble.

00:45:00.350 --> 00:45:02.100
The point, I don't know.

00:45:02.880 --> 00:45:06.320
And that is Macy's stupefying addition to the sum of human knowledge.

00:45:08.140 --> 00:45:09.580
Well, not quite.

00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:13.220
Macy also happens to be the illegitimate son

00:45:13.220 --> 00:45:15.420
of one of the oldest aristocratic families in England,

00:45:15.560 --> 00:45:16.740
the Dukes of Northumberland.

00:45:16.880 --> 00:45:18.640
So the boy's socially well-placed,

00:45:19.200 --> 00:45:21.490
as a result of which, he ends up quite rich.

00:45:22.240 --> 00:45:24.300
And since he never marries, he leaves it to a cousin,

00:45:24.500 --> 00:45:27.650
unless the cousin dies without children, which he does.

00:45:27.980 --> 00:45:29.970
In which case, all the money is to...

00:45:30.000 --> 00:45:36.450
go to America and there for a moment we leave James Macy. Now we return to moss

00:45:36.450 --> 00:45:41.200
collecting Macintosh. Remember him? One of his early partners in the cloth dyeing

00:45:41.200 --> 00:45:47.270
business, David Dale, builds a textile mill at New Lanark in Scotland where he

00:45:47.270 --> 00:45:51.490
philanthropically employs orphaned children aged seven or so to run the

00:45:51.490 --> 00:45:59.980
machines. Back then this is reckoned to be do-good stuff because it saves the kids.

00:46:00.000 --> 00:46:01.840
from starving to death in the streets.

00:46:02.520 --> 00:46:03.920
Dale even builds him a school.

00:46:04.430 --> 00:46:06.270
And they get clean clothes once a week.

00:46:07.260 --> 00:46:09.020
In return for such generosity,

00:46:09.340 --> 00:46:11.040
the deal with the orphanage is

00:46:11.040 --> 00:46:13.120
Dale doesn't have to pay any wages

00:46:13.120 --> 00:46:16.100
to his little employees until they're age 15.

00:46:16.850 --> 00:46:18.350
What a sweetheart deal!

00:46:19.770 --> 00:46:21.530
Now, Dale's liberal attitude

00:46:21.620 --> 00:46:23.920
attracts a young socialist mill manager

00:46:23.920 --> 00:46:26.770
called Robert Owen, who marries Dale's daughter

00:46:26.770 --> 00:46:28.190
and takes over the mill.

00:46:29.200 --> 00:46:29.960
In 1825...

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:36.220
before he leaves here for America, where he sets up a commune at a place called New Harmony, Indiana.

00:46:39.910 --> 00:46:42.930
Well, the venture fails, and he comes back here to Britain.

00:46:43.720 --> 00:46:47.680
But his four sons take out American citizenship and stay on in the States,

00:46:48.460 --> 00:46:53.940
where their story and that of James Macy, the rich bamboo freak, join up.

00:46:57.260 --> 00:46:59.970
This is where it all comes together, and when things get...

00:47:00.000 --> 00:47:02.480
to hit the fan in a really spectacular manner.

00:47:03.850 --> 00:47:07.840
See, Owen's eldest son is a fellow called Robert Dale Owen,

00:47:08.040 --> 00:47:13.100
and in 1842, he gets elected to Congress as an Indiana Democrat.

00:47:14.640 --> 00:47:19.380
Well, in 1844, he gets mixed up in the affairs of an Englishman called James Macy.

00:47:19.700 --> 00:47:23.700
Remember him? He's the fellow who did all that weird work on bamboo juice.

00:47:24.300 --> 00:47:29.980
And you remember he left a fortune of 104,960 of these...

00:47:30.000 --> 00:47:37.090
things, English gold sovereigns, to America? Well, by 1844, they've arrived in the States,

00:47:37.290 --> 00:47:42.530
been melted down, and turned into US money. And when I tell you how much money, you'll

00:47:42.530 --> 00:47:50.690
know why I said things are about to hit the fan. Two billion and change. Suddenly, it's

00:47:50.690 --> 00:47:59.740
Washington behind closed doors time. Turns out, as soon as the money is available in greenbacks,

00:48:00.480 --> 00:48:03.980
The Treasury has invested no less than $2 billion worth of it

00:48:03.980 --> 00:48:06.220
in this interesting outfit,

00:48:06.580 --> 00:48:10.080
known as the Real Estate Bank of the state of Arkansas.

00:48:11.470 --> 00:48:12.350
Let me put it this way.

00:48:13.050 --> 00:48:15.610
There are criminal charges against some of the bank's directors.

00:48:16.570 --> 00:48:18.110
Somebody involved has been knifed.

00:48:18.750 --> 00:48:21.370
Above all, everybody knows the bank's going to go bust

00:48:21.370 --> 00:48:25.870
because its real estate has been grossly and deliberately overvalued.

00:48:27.360 --> 00:48:29.640
And the US government is in bed with these people?

00:48:35.240 --> 00:48:38.990
Never mind which moron in the Treasury okayed the deal.

00:48:39.560 --> 00:48:42.660
By this time, there are now two and a half a billion dollars

00:48:42.660 --> 00:48:44.780
invested in these examples of junk.

00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:47.200
And how's the Treasury going to get their hands on it?

00:48:47.500 --> 00:48:51.240
Never mind the interest the Real Estate Bank of Arkansas hasn't paid.

00:48:52.620 --> 00:48:55.160
Well, you can imagine the kind of field day

00:48:55.160 --> 00:48:59.730
the legislators up here on the hill have with this little peccadillo.

00:49:01.340 --> 00:49:04.980
Gentlemen from Massachusetts, John Quincy Adams, to be exact,

00:49:05.480 --> 00:49:07.340
make caustic comments about theft.

00:49:08.410 --> 00:49:11.030
Gentlemen from Arkansas make comments about honour

00:49:11.030 --> 00:49:14.150
and meeting their commitments in due course.

00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:18.460
Others wonder when that distant day might be.

00:49:19.900 --> 00:49:21.160
The Arkansas people say,

00:49:21.390 --> 00:49:27.000
in 20 years, when the bonds mature, Arkansas will repay its debts.

00:49:27.830 --> 00:49:29.970
Finally, it's agreed the Treasury will...

00:49:30.000 --> 00:49:36.690
Guarantee the interest so the money can do what James Macy wanted it to do and then there's a real row

00:49:37.480 --> 00:49:38.620
certain members

00:49:38.620 --> 00:49:47.490
Regard the gift as an insult to the American people and say that the United States has humbled and

00:49:47.520 --> 00:49:51.740
Degraded itself by accepting this money from degenerate

00:49:51.740 --> 00:49:58.860
Aristocrats others argue that the United States should take the money and run

00:50:00.000 --> 00:50:03.860
Eventually, common sense prevails, and in December 1846,

00:50:04.140 --> 00:50:06.740
Owen's bill is passed, and they get to spend the money

00:50:06.740 --> 00:50:07.780
the way Macy wanted.

00:50:08.800 --> 00:50:09.920
You know how, of course.

00:50:10.700 --> 00:50:12.480
And if you don't, I'll tell you in a minute.

00:50:13.410 --> 00:50:16.680
So, winding up this debate, let me summarise.

00:50:18.130 --> 00:50:22.030
Corn cobs become resins for diamond polishing with carborundum

00:50:22.030 --> 00:50:24.620
that protects you from shells fired at tanks

00:50:24.620 --> 00:50:26.500
developed from American tractors

00:50:26.500 --> 00:50:29.650
that used diesel engines built with funding from Krupp.

00:50:30.120 --> 00:50:33.500
who inspired Bismarck's welfare scheme based on Ketelay's statistics

00:50:33.500 --> 00:50:38.320
that inspired the Babbage engine whose punch cards were used to rivet the Great Eastern.

00:50:38.510 --> 00:50:42.410
The monster ship that laid the transatlantic cable insulated with gutter percha,

00:50:42.850 --> 00:50:47.050
used to manufacture golf balls for factory managers in industrial Scotland,

00:50:47.390 --> 00:50:51.810
where James Watt had a run-in with Cavendish, whose protégé was James Macy,

00:50:54.600 --> 00:50:59.960
who caused all the row here in the Capitol building for reasons you know, but maybe don't...

00:51:00.000 --> 00:51:09.220
know, you know. Get it? You remember I said James Macy was the illegitimate son of the Duke of

00:51:09.220 --> 00:51:14.840
Northumberland. Well, after his father's death, James took on the father's family name. Well,

00:51:14.940 --> 00:51:22.470
what's in a name? So the money got used to set up a world-renowned institution named after James

00:51:22.470 --> 00:51:29.010
Macy's new family name, which was Smithson, the institution known as the Smithsonian.
